{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue #31:\n1. \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n2. \"npm test passes\"\n\nContext you need first: today src/config.js is `return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));`. src/app.js already wraps loadConfig in try/catch and prints `bad config: <message>` then exits 1, and README.md documents that behaviour. test/config.test.js has a test 'bad JSON throws' that expects a SyntaxError. The fix the issue asks for changes all of that, because invalid JSON will no longer throw.\n\nSteps:\n1. In src/config.js, read the file with readFileSync outside the try, then put only the JSON.parse call inside a try/catch. When the parse throws, return {}. Do not catch readFileSync errors (a missing file should still throw as it does now); catch only the parse error.\n2. The issue says people need to know which file is broken and where. Returning {} on its own hides that, so in the catch also write one line to stderr that names the path and includes the parse error message (which holds the position), e.g. `bad config: <path>: <error.message>\\n`. Keep the return value exactly {}.\n3. Update test/config.test.js: replace 'bad JSON throws' with a test that loadConfig on a file holding `{port: 9000` returns a value deepEqual to {} and does not throw. Keep 'reads a config'. If you can do it simply, add a test that a missing file still throws.\n4. Update README.md so it matches the new behaviour: invalid JSON prints a warning naming the file and the app starts with the defaults. Leave the try/catch in src/app.js as it is; it still handles read errors.\n5. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure every test passes.\n6. In the PR description, quote both acceptance lines, and say plainly that with this change a broken config.json no longer stops the app with exit 1: it starts on the defaults after printing a warning. A reviewer should know that before merging.","verifier":"Check the PR head against the two acceptance lines of issue #31, in this order. Run everything from the repository root at the PR head.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun:\n  d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/config.json\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig(process.argv[1]); console.log(JSON.stringify(r), Object.getPrototypeOf(r) === Object.prototype, Object.keys(r).length);\" \"$d/config.json\"; echo exit=$?\nAlso open src/config.js and confirm that loadConfig is the function exported there and that it calls JSON.parse.\nPASS: the command does not throw, stdout contains `{} true 0`, and exit=0 (stderr may hold a warning line).\nFAIL: it throws (a SyntaxError or anything else), exits non-zero, prints anything other than `{} true 0` as the result line, or loadConfig is not in src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test; echo exit=$?\nPASS: exit=0 and the node --test summary shows fail 0.\nFAIL: a non-zero exit, or any test reported as failed.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS or FAIL):\n- Check that a valid file still loads: the same command with `{\"port\": 9000}` should print `{\"port\":9000} ...`.\n- Check that only the parse error is caught: loadConfig on a path that does not exist should still throw ENOENT.\n- Check whether a warning naming the file path and the parse position goes to stderr. The issue body asks for this, and without it a broken config is silently ignored.\n- `node src/app.js \"$d/config.json\"` will now print `listening on 8080` and exit 0 instead of `bad config: ...` and exit 1. Check that README.md and the PR description say so."}